---
/**
 * Layer 1 of the insurance analyser. The answer, as a sentence.
 *
 * The page used to lead with the policy's XIRR - the answer to an analyst's
 * question - and left the reader to assemble the one they actually came with
 * out of two cards, a badge and a bottom line that contradicted each other.
 *
 * The single comparison this whole subject reduces to (dd-005) is: buy the
 * policy, or buy the cover on its own and invest the difference? Everything
 * else on this page is a push on one side of it.
 *
 * The design note that matters is in InsuranceAnswer.checks.md, written before
 * this file: the DIY route is DEFINED to fund the identical payouts in the
 * identical years, so the income is held constant by construction and the only
 * thing left to compare is what remains at the end. That is said on the screen,
 * in those words - without it a reader cannot tell whether the surplus was
 * bought by cutting their income.
 *
 * Every figure here is rendered by insurance-analyzer.astro from the one
 * `analyseReplication` result. This component holds no state and reads no form.
 */
---

<section
	id="insuranceAnswer"
	class="rounded-2xl border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800 bg-white dark:bg-navy-950 p-6 sm:p-8"
	aria-live="polite"
>
	<div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 border-b border-navy-100 dark:border-navy-800 pb-4 mb-6">
		<h2 class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-300">
			Should you buy this policy?
		</h2>
		<span
			id="answerBadge"
			class="shrink-0 px-2 py-0.5 text-[9px] font-bold tracking-tight rounded bg-navy-100 dark:bg-navy-800 text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400"
			>Evaluating</span
		>
	</div>

	<!--
		dd-008: the values live in fixed slots the eye can return to, and the prose
		around them stays true at every position of every input. Nothing here is
		rewritten as a control moves; only the slots change.
	-->
	<p
		id="answerHeadline"
		class="text-lg sm:text-xl leading-relaxed text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100"
	>
		Evaluating this policy against buying the cover on its own…
	</p>

	<!--
		The premise the comparison rests on. Fixed text on purpose: it is a
		principle, not a state (dd-008), and without it the surplus below is
		unreadable - the reader has no way to know it was not bought by cutting
		their own income.
	-->
	<p class="mt-4 text-sm text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400 leading-relaxed">
		Both routes pay you <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100"
			>the same income, in the same years</strong
		>. The DIY route is built to fund every rupee this policy promises, so the
		only thing left to compare is what is left over at the end — and what the
		protection costs to keep.
	</p>

	<!--
		T8 / dd-004. The two moneys sit next to each other, permanently, with the
		frame carried on the figure itself. Not a toggle (dd-006), not a footnote
		(dd-004/dont-1), and never the words "real" or "nominal".
	-->
	<div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
		<div class="rounded-lg border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800 bg-navy-50 dark:bg-navy-900 p-4">
			<div id="answerFigureLabel" class="text-[10px] font-bold tracking-tight text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400">
				Left over at the end
			</div>
			<div class="mt-2 flex flex-col gap-1">
				<div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2">
					<span id="answerFigureNominal" class="text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100">₹0</span>
					<span class="text-[10px] tracking-tight text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400">in the rupees of that year</span>
				</div>
				<div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2 pt-1 border-t border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800">
					<span id="answerFigureReal" class="text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100">₹0</span>
					<span class="text-[10px] tracking-tight text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400">in today's prices</span>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="rounded-lg border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800 bg-navy-50 dark:bg-navy-900 p-4">
			<div class="text-[10px] font-bold tracking-tight text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400">
				The policy pays you back at
			</div>
			<div class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100">
				<span id="answerYield">0.00%</span> <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400">a year</span>
			</div>
			<p id="answerYieldNote" class="mt-2 text-xs text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400 leading-relaxed">&nbsp;</p>
		</div>

		<div class="rounded-lg border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800 bg-navy-50 dark:bg-navy-900 p-4">
			<div class="text-[10px] font-bold tracking-tight text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400">
				What the cover costs
			</div>
			<div class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100">
				<span id="answerCoverCost">₹0</span>
			</div>
			<p id="answerCoverNote" class="mt-2 text-xs text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400 leading-relaxed">&nbsp;</p>
		</div>
	</div>

	<!--
		dd-017. A level premium is a ten-year commitment quoted in flat nominal
		rupees, so it gets quietly cheaper every year - which flatters the policy.
		The page never said so. It does now.
	-->
	<p id="answerPremiumDecay" class="mt-5 text-xs text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400 leading-relaxed">
		&nbsp;
	</p>
</section>
